{
  "name": "console-legacy-input",
  "profile": "kr-vt/1",
  "description": "KR-REQ-08.53. The legacy VT input a session is sent for the console's key records when its backend did not ask for them, written by hand from xterm's control sequence documentation and the console API's key state. Every field of every record is written. A case with `bytes` is the exact spelling, and an empty `bytes` is a record that spells nothing; a case with `refused` is a write the worker must not spell, because the application negotiated an encoding legacy input cannot express. `keyboard` is the session's state: DEC mode 1, and the keyboard protocol negotiated. The records have the six fields `input-encodings.json` gives each of its own.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "text-repeated",
      "covers": "KR-REQ-08.53",
      "why": "A held character is that many characters, as a terminal's translation would have sent.",
